{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "**Chasing Sakura: A Whirlwind Week in Japan**\n\nLanding in Tokyo felt like stepping into a living postcard. Neon signs flickered over Shibuya Crossing while the scent of yakitori drifted from tiny alley stalls. My first morning began at the Meiji Shrine, where towering cedars whispered prayers and visitors tossed coins into the offering box, hoping for luck. A quick ride on the Yamanote Line brought me to Harajuku, where crepe stands lined Takeshita‑dori and fashion‑forward teens strutted past vintage boutiques.\n\nDay two was all about history. I wandered the serene gardens of the Imperial Palace, then hopped on the shinkansen to Kyoto. The bullet train glided past rice fields and mist‑capped mountains, arriving just in time for sunset at Kiyomizu‑dera. The wooden terrace offered a panoramic view of the city’s tiled roofs, ablaze with autumn hues.\n\nKyoto’s temples became my playground. I meditated beneath the moss‑covered stones of Ryoan‑ji, sipped matcha in a traditional tea house near Gion, and chased geisha silhouettes along Hanami‑koji Street. A spontaneous detour to Nara rewarded me with friendly deer bowing for crackers at Todai‑ji, where the Great Buddha sat serenely amidst towering wooden pillars.\n\nThe final leg took me to Osaka, the nation’s kitchen. Dotonbori’s neon canal reflected a kaleidoscope of lights as I sampled takoyaki, okonomiyaki, and melt‑in‑your‑mouth kushikatsu. Late‑night karaoke bars let me belt out J‑pop hits with locals who cheered each off‑key note.\n\nSeven days blurred into a tapestry of ancient tradition and futuristic buzz. From the quiet contemplation of Zen gardens to the electric pulse of city streets, Japan reminded me that travel is as much about the moments between sights as the sights themselves. I left with a suitcase full of souvenirs, a heart full of sakura‑scented memories, and a promise to retu